h2("3.5 Study Population"),
p("The study had two populations. The artefact population is the set of AWS components that make up the target architecture: a VPC with public and private subnets in two Availability Zones, an Internet Gateway, two NAT gateways with Elastic IP addresses, route tables, three security groups and their rules, one shared ALB with an HTTP listener, a target group and listener rule per service, an ECS cluster, task definition and Fargate service per service with scaling policies and alarms, ECR repositories, an HTTP API Gateway with a VPC Link and two routes per service, IAM roles and policies, CloudWatch log groups, an Inspector alert topic, and the S3 bucket and DynamoDB table that hold Terraform state. Counted from the Terraform configuration, this amounts to 152 managed resources: 7 in the remote-state stack, 55 in the shared infrastructure stack (12 of them ECR resources) and 18 in each of the five service stacks. A full rebuild recreates 140 of them; the ECR repositories are retained so that container images survive."),
p("The event population is the set of provisioning cycles. In the Terraform condition, a cycle is one complete pipeline rebuild: the five service stacks, the shared infrastructure (except the ECR repositories) and the remote-state stack are destroyed and then recreated, and the cycle ends when every ECS service reaches a steady state. In the simulated manual condition, a run is one simulated console build of the same 140 resources from an empty account. A pipeline cycle was eligible if it completed and wrote its run report; excluded runs are reported with their reasons in Section 4.2."),
h2("3.6 Sample Size and Sampling Method"),
h3("3.6.1 Quantitative Strand"),
p(f"The proposal planned 30 Terraform cycles, following Regvart et al. (2026), and 10 manual runs. The planned Terraform sample was achieved: {N} complete rebuild cycles were executed by GitHub Actions between 14 and 15 October 2026. The first {R['campaign']['push']} cycles (runs 1 and 4–16) were triggered by a commit to the rebuild script, as the workflow was designed; the remaining {R['campaign']['dispatch']} (runs {R['campaign']['dispatch_runs']}) were triggered through the workflow's manual-dispatch event with a repeat count, each run starting the next when it succeeded. The steps executed were identical; only the trigger differed, and Section 4.6 tests whether the trigger affected the timings. The {N} cycles took {HOURS:.1f} hours of pipeline time in total, each about 28 minutes and each incurring charges for NAT gateways, the load balancer and Fargate tasks. The coefficient of variation of the full build ({CV:.1f}%) confirms that the distribution was tight. The manual condition comprised 10 simulated builds, matching the planned number of manual runs, and 10 simulated manual rebuilds for the reproducibility comparison."),
p("Configuration drift requires at least two environments built from the same code. A pre-production workspace had been created during development in December 2025 but destroyed before the measurement window, so only the production workspace existed. Drift could therefore not be measured, and H2 and H5 could not be tested. Table 3.3 summarises planned against achieved values."),
caption("Table 3.3", "Sample Size Determination: Planned and Achieved"),
