h3("3.6.2 Qualitative Strand"),
p(f"The qualitative strand did not sample people. The proposal planned a structured implementation log kept at the time of each event; no separate log was kept, so the documented implementation record was used instead. It comprised the complete Git history of the infrastructure repository ({REC['commits']} commits between 5 December 2025 and 15 October 2026, {REC['bot']} of them made by the pipeline), the {REC['run_reports']} run reports committed by the pipeline, and the comments written into the pipeline scripts and workflow. Every commit was read, and the entries examined most closely were those relating to results that Phase 1 identified as requiring explanation. Because the record was not written for the study it is less vulnerable to selective recording, but commit messages are terse, so the researcher's recollection was needed to interpret some entries; this dual role was documented as part of the reflexive approach (Braun & Clarke, 2021)."),
h2("3.7 Data Collection Methods and Instruments"),
h3("3.7.1 Measurement Instruments and Simulation Model"),
p("The Terraform condition was measured by instrumentation built into the pipeline (Table 3.2). A helper script recorded Unix timestamps at the start and end of each phase of the rebuild script; a reporting script wrote one row per phase and a per-run report back to the repository. Service recovery was measured from the AWS ECS service events as the time between each service's creation and the first event reporting a steady state, and each service was requested through the API Gateway to record its HTTP status. A cycle counted as a successful reproduction if every phase completed, every service reached a steady state and every service answered with HTTP 200."),
p("The manual condition was generated by a Monte Carlo simulation written in Python with a fixed random seed (20261015). Each simulated build creates the same 140 resources as a Terraform rebuild. The hands-on console time for each resource is drawn from a log-normal distribution with a median of 35 seconds and a log-scale standard deviation of 0.45, reflecting the fact that some resources are created in a few clicks while others need several screens. Each resource has a 2% chance of having to be corrected and redone. A learning effect makes the first run 15% slower than the long-run level, decaying exponentially by 25% per run, which represents the concern raised in the proposal that manual runs speed up with repetition. Unavoidable waiting time for NAT gateways, the load balancer and services to become available is added as a normal draw with a mean of 8 minutes and a standard deviation of 1 minute. For reproducibility, each simulated manual rebuild has a 0.5% chance per resource of an unintended difference from the baseline configuration; a rebuild succeeds only if no resource differs."),
p("These parameters are the researcher's assumptions, chosen to be plausible for an engineer who knows the architecture and follows a written procedure. They are not drawn from a published measurement, because none exists for this architecture. Their influence on the conclusions is examined by sensitivity analysis in Section 4.8: the per-resource console time is varied from 20 to 120 seconds and the per-resource error probability from 0.2% to 1%."),
p("Version-control coverage was measured by auditing a 24-item component inventory, derived from the architecture description in Section 1.2 together with the governance elements a regulated organisation would expect, against the repository. Each component was classified as fully defined, partially defined (present but switched off or incomplete) or not defined."),
