h2("3.11 Ethical Considerations"),
p("This study involved no human participants, so the consent and confidentiality procedures that dominate the template's corresponding section did not apply in their usual form. Confirmation that no ethical approval was required is to be recorded in Appendix D. Five ethical matters did arise."),
p("The first is organisational confidentiality: the case organisation is not named, and account identifiers, internal addresses and policy documents are not reproduced. The second is data protection: all work was performed in an isolated AWS account holding no real data. The third is credential handling: the proposal committed to short-lived OpenID Connect credentials, but the implemented workflow authenticated with a long-lived access key stored as an encrypted repository secret; no credential was written to the repository, and the deviation is addressed in the recommendations (Guerriero et al., 2019; Rahman, Parnin, & Williams, 2019). The fourth is cost: each cycle destroyed the previous environment before rebuilding it, so only one environment was billed at a time, and the planned 30 cycles were run within two days to bound the charges."),
p("The fifth is research integrity in the use of simulated data. Presenting simulated values as observations would be falsification. The manual condition is therefore identified as simulated in the abstract, in this chapter, and in every table and figure in which it appears; the model and its parameters are published with the analysis code; and the conclusions drawn from simulated comparisons are worded as conditional on the model."),
h2("3.12 Methodological Limitations"),
p("Six limitations follow from the design and its execution. First, this is a single case, so generalisation is analytical rather than statistical. Second, and most important, the manual condition was simulated: the H1 and H3 comparisons show what follows from the stated model, and their strength depends on its assumptions. Third, the Terraform cycles were run within two days, so they sample AWS and runner conditions over a short window, and the last sixteen were triggered differently from the first fourteen. Fourth, only one environment existed, so configuration drift, H2 and H5 could not be tested. Fifth, the gated-apply level of integration was not implemented, so integration depth could not be varied. Sixth, the researcher built, measured, modelled and interpreted the system, which bears on both strands. These limitations are revisited in Chapter 6 with recommendations for designs that could address them."),
h2("3.13 Chapter Summary"),
p(f"This chapter set out the methodology as conducted. A pragmatist philosophy justified an abductive approach and an explanatory sequential mixed methods design. Thirty Terraform rebuilds executed by GitHub Actions were measured by pipeline instrumentation and compared with ten simulated manual console builds generated by a stated, seeded model; a component audit measured coverage. The implementation record of {REC['commits']} commits and {REC['run_reports']} run reports was analysed by reflexive thematic analysis, and the strands were integrated in a joint display. Deviations from the proposal (simulated rather than observed manual runs, one environment and no gated apply) were stated with their consequences. The research timeline as conducted is presented in Table 3.5."),
